# PerfectMarkD configuration
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Fill in the four secrets — the stack will not start without them — plus
# ADMIN_EMAIL so you can log in as Admin, and the three mail values, which the
# API refuses to start without. Everything else has a working default.
#
# This file is never committed. Do not paste real keys into it.

# ── Secrets ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Generate all four with:
#
#   for k in SESSION_SECRET HISTORY_ENCRYPTION_KEY UMAMI_APP_SECRET \
#            UMAMI_DB_PASSWORD; do echo "$k=$(openssl rand -hex 32)"; done

# Signs login cookies.
SESSION_SECRET=

# Encrypts Premium Export History at rest. Changing it after exports exist
# makes every previously exported file permanently unreadable.
HISTORY_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Signs Umami's own login tokens.
UMAMI_APP_SECRET=

# Password for the analytics database.
UMAMI_DB_PASSWORD=

# ── Your instance ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The first account registered with this address becomes the Admin. Register
# with exactly this address or nothing gets the flag.
ADMIN_EMAIL=

# AI Actions: your provider's API key. Leave it empty and the AI features do
# not exist in the UI at all. Changing it needs an api restart.
# The model, limits and kill switch are set in Admin → Settings, not here.
AI_API_KEY=

# Google Sign-In: the OAuth client id and secret from your own Google project,
# so a person can register or sign in with their Google account and never
# invent a password here. Optional like AI_API_KEY — leave both empty and the
# feature does not exist: no button on the sign-in page, no routes on the api.
# Changing any of it needs an api restart.
#
# Create the client in the Google Cloud Console (APIs & Services → OAuth
# client ID → Web application), and register BOTH of these as Authorized
# redirect URIs:
#
#   https://perfectmarkd.00022000.xyz/auth/google/callback   (production)
#   http://localhost:3000/auth/google/callback                (development)
#
# The scopes asked for are openid, email and profile only — nothing sensitive,
# so no Google verification audit. GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI picks which of the two
# this deployment sends the consent screen back to; leave it unset for
# production, and point it at your own API when you run the servers locally.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
#GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=https://perfectmarkd.00022000.xyz/auth/google/callback

# Transactional email: the provider's API key and the address it sends from.
# Not optional like AI_API_KEY — sign-in is blocked until an address is
# verified, so the API refuses to start without these two. The key belongs to
# the deployment: environment only, never a setting, never in a log. Rotating it
# needs an api restart. MAIL_FROM takes the form the provider expects, on a
# domain you have verified there (with its DKIM/SPF records) or nothing is
# delivered — e.g. PerfectMarkD <hello@perfectmarkd.00022000.xyz>
RESEND_API_KEY=
MAIL_FROM=

# The address users reach your instance on, e.g.
# https://perfectmarkd.example.com. The verification link in the email above is
# absolute, and nothing in the server can work it out from a request — so this
# is required too, and the API refuses to start without it. It is NOT the
# internal EXPORT_ORIGIN (http://caddy): that one is the loopback address the
# export worker loads /export from, and a link pointing at it is dead in a real
# inbox. No trailing slash needed; behind a Cloudflare Tunnel, put the public
# https domain here, not SITE_ADDRESS.
PUBLIC_ORIGIN=

# To run the API locally with no provider account, set MAIL_MODE=console:
# transactional email is printed to the server log instead of sent, links and
# all. It is the only way past the gate above, so it has to be asked for by
# name — leave it unset on a real instance.
#MAIL_MODE=console

# Analytics website id, copied from Umami. Leave empty at first — you get it
# once the stack is up, following the Analytics section of the README. Empty
# means no analytics.
ANALYTICS_WEBSITE_ID=

# ── Only if the defaults don't fit your machine ───────────────────────────────

# Where Caddy publishes. 80 suits most hosts; if something already serves :80
# (nginx, another stack), give Caddy a different port and point your tunnel
# or proxy at it.
CADDY_HTTP_PORT=8901

# The HTTPS port, published next to it. Under rootless podman this must be 1024
# or higher: the kernel refuses unprivileged binds below that, so the default 443
# fails with "rootlessport cannot expose privileged port 443". To keep 443
# itself, set net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start=443 in /etc/sysctl.d and run
# `sudo sysctl --system`. Caddy only listens here once SITE_ADDRESS names a
# domain — the default ":80" is plain HTTP.
CADDY_HTTPS_PORT=8443

# Simultaneous Server Export renders. Each one is a headless Chromium needing
# roughly a gigabyte, and the default is two at once. Set this to 1 on a
# machine with less than about 4 GB free.
#EXPORT_CONCURRENCY=2

# ── Backups, off by default ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Nothing at all is backed up until this is 1. With backups off there is no
# way to recover your database or your Export History.
#BACKUP_ENABLED=0

# Where to upload. Leave empty to keep the backup on this machine only —
# which still covers accidents, but not losing the machine.
#BACKUP_REMOTE=b2:perfectmarkd-backups

# ── Rarely changed ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Pin the exact api and caddy images this deployment runs. `latest` follows
# every push to main; a `sha-…` tag pins a known-good pair; a version tag
# (0.1.0) pins a release. The version those images report is the `version`
# field in package.json, stamped in at build time — see README § Versions and
# releases. `curl -s localhost:8901/healthz` tells you what is actually up.
#PERFECTMARKD_IMAGE_TAG=latest

# Set this to a domain only for a host exposed directly to the internet. Leave
# it alone behind a tunnel, which terminates TLS itself.
#SITE_ADDRESS=:80

# ── Development only ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Docker Compose sets its own database paths, so these stay commented out
# unless you are running the servers directly with `pnpm dev`.
#PORT=3000
#DB_PATH=./data/perfectmarkd.db
#HISTORY_DIR=./data/history
